From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: sunxi: add the H713 HDMI receiver as a V4L2 capture device

The H713 has an HDMI input, and nothing in the kernel can reach it. There
is no HDMI receiver register block for the ARM: the receiver, the picture
pipeline behind it and the capture DMA all belong to the SoC's MIPS
co-processor, which is asked over the cpu_comm IPC, while the hotplug pin
and the EDID store belong to a second co-processor, the ARISC. Bringing
the input up has so far meant running a sequence of shell and python
scripts against /dev/cpu_comm and /dev/mem by hand.

Add sun50i-h713-hdmirx: /dev/videoN, with the bring-up sequence in the
probe and the three capture slots as buffers.

The bring-up sequence is a table, not code. It is the stock initialisation
in stock order -- twenty-two calls, with the stock RPC session number of
each one next to it -- because the order is not a convention, it is what
the firmware accepts, and prose invites reordering. One stock call is
deliberately missing: SetWhiteBalance takes a pointer to three factory
calibrated doubles that are not available, and the sequence works without
it.

Three properties of this hardware shape the rest:

The capture target is not programmable. The firmware writes NV16 into
three fixed slot pairs inside its own framebuffer reservation and rotates
a pair of pointers through them. A buffer therefore cannot be allocated:
buffer i *is* slot i, handed to userspace by mmap through a small memory
operations set, and QBUF/DQBUF only pass ownership. Nothing is copied.
The slot addresses are checked against the reserved region the device tree
names, rather than being trusted.

There is no capture interrupt on the ARM side. Three independent searches
through the firmware image found the "frame done" interrupt only inside
the MIPS interrupt controller, and the stock device tree wires no capture
interrupt to the ARM either; the two ring pointers are advanced by
hardware, not by firmware code. What the ARM does have is the display's
vsync, GIC 142, which sun50i-h713-afbd services and where it already reads
that pointer pair for its own passthrough plane. This driver takes its
"slot finished" events from there, through that driver's flip notifier,
behind a small slot-source interface, so a real capture interrupt could
still take the job over one day without touching the queue handling.

That the display driver is the one to read the pair is not a convenience,
it is the only thing that works: the pair lives at 0x05600320, eight bytes
inside the 0x400 register window display@5600000 owns and claims, and
/proc/iomem reads "05600000-056003ff : 5600000.display afbd". A reg of its
own here plus devm_of_iomap() -- which calls devm_ioremap_resource() and
does claim the region, whatever a comment saying "mapped, not claimed"
promised -- returns -EBUSY and the probe fails outright. So the mapping is
gone, and with it the reg property and the node's unit address; what is
left has no registers at all and therefore sits beside sound rather than in
the soc bus, where a node without reg draws a dtc simple_bus_reg warning.

Gone with it is the 120 Hz timer that used to sample the pair, and it is
not kept as a fallback behind a module parameter. It sampled at a rate
unrelated to the event it was looking for: half its wakeups found nothing
and the other half found a slot that had been finished up to 8 ms earlier.
A sampling clock ticking quietly beside the real event is a quirk, and
there is no configuration in which it would be the better of the two --
without the display driver there is no plane, no frame descriptor and no
ring bounds either, so there is nothing to fall back to. Kconfig says so
too, and the probe reports it: -EPROBE_DEFER while display@5600000 has not
bound yet, a real error if the driver is not there at all.

Signal loss and signal return are announced by the firmware itself,
through its SignalChange callback; that is measured, both directions. The
V4L2 source change event hangs off that callback and nothing polls for it.
The two callbacks are registered by name, not by hashed id, because
registering them is what puts their descriptors into the cpu_comm routine
table -- and until the descriptor is there the firmware's own name lookup
fails and it sends nothing, whatever the receiver has armed.

The same fact says when to register, and the answer is the opposite of
what the userspace bring-up script does: last, not first. The descriptor
is what arms the sender, so from the moment it is in the table the
firmware can call in on the cpu_comm receive work item, in parallel with
whatever the driver does next. Registering at the top of the probe puts
that call in the middle of the bring-up chain -- twenty-two synchronous
RPCs to the MIPS, then the ARISC EDID and hotplug sequence -- and one
delivered callback in there was enough to break the probe on 07.09., in a
different place on each of two cold starts. So the registration happens
after video_register_device(), where the driver is idle again. Nothing is
lost by that: both handlers end in a V4L2 source-change event, events go
to subscribed file handles, and before the video node exists there is no
handle to subscribe.

What the firmware detected, though, is not readable: the signal record it
points at is 44 bytes of which one field is decoded so far, so
QUERY_DV_TIMINGS reports the one timing the whole pipeline is built for
and reports it only while the ring pointers are actually moving, which is
a measurement rather than a cached flag. It asks the display driver for
the pointers for that, too. The raw record is in debugfs so that decoding
it takes one run on hardware, and so is a count of the vsync events this
driver has been handed, which is what says on the device that the notifier
is carrying.

The driver has been compile-checked in a test build of the whole series,
with W=1 and no warnings, and the device tree compiles with no new
warnings. On hardware it needs the ARISC driver and the cpu_comm
in-kernel API on the board beside it; the acceptance run is written out in
doku/88-v4l2-hdmirx.md.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff -ruN a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi	2026-09-07 09:22:33.397102793 +0200
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi	2026-09-07 09:22:34.642673757 +0200
@@ -1903,6 +1903,47 @@
 		};
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * HDMI receiver, exposed as a V4L2 capture device. OURS, not in
+	 * cstenger's tree.
+	 *
+	 * The node has no registers, which is why it sits here beside sound and
+	 * not in the soc bus: there is no HDMI receiver register block for the
+	 * ARM on this SoC. The receiver, the picture pipeline behind it and the
+	 * capture DMA belong to the MIPS co-processor; the driver reaches the
+	 * pipeline over cpu_comm (THal_Vp_* calls) and the hotplug pin and EDID
+	 * store over the ARISC, and it learns which capture slot is finished
+	 * from the flip notifier of display@5600000 -- that node owns the
+	 * register pair, claims it, and reads it on every vsync anyway. All
+	 * three are dependencies in Kconfig, not resources here. What is left
+	 * to describe is the memory the capture lands in.
+	 *
+	 * Binding:
+	 *   compatible     "allwinner,sun50i-h713-hdmirx"
+	 *   memory-region  two regions, by name:
+	 *     "hdmi-ring"       the firmware's framebuffer reservation. The
+	 *                       three Y and three C capture slots live inside
+	 *                       it; the driver checks its slot addresses
+	 *                       against the region at probe instead of
+	 *                       trusting them.
+	 *     "cpu-comm-shmem"  the IPC shared memory. Call arguments that are
+	 *                       pointers -- the HDCP key, the two window
+	 *                       rectangles, the Vp_Init staging area -- have to
+	 *                       live where the MIPS can address them, and this
+	 *                       is that place. Named, not hard coded.
+	 *
+	 * No interrupt: three independent searches of the firmware image found
+	 * the capture interrupt only inside the MIPS interrupt controller, and
+	 * the stock device tree wires none to the ARM either (doku/84, RE
+	 * question K2).
+	 */
+	hdmirx: hdmi-rx {
+		compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h713-hdmirx";
+		memory-region = <&mips_framebuf>, <&cpu_comm_reserved>;
+		memory-region-names = "hdmi-ring", "cpu-comm-shmem";
+		status = "okay";
+	};
+
 	codec_machine: sound {
 		compatible = "allwinner,sunxi-codec-machine";
 		interrupts = <GIC_SPI 25 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/Kconfig b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/Kconfig	2026-07-04 13:44:22.000000000 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/Kconfig	2026-09-07 09:22:34.642856057 +0200
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@
 comment "Sunxi media platform drivers"
 
 source "drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun4i-csi/Kconfig"
+source "drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Kconfig"
 source "drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun6i-csi/Kconfig"
 source "drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun6i-mipi-csi2/Kconfig"
 source "drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun8i-a83t-mipi-csi2/Kconfig"
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/Makefile b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/Makefile
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/Makefile	2026-07-04 13:44:22.000000000 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/Makefile	2026-09-07 09:22:34.642892998 +0200
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
 
 obj-y		+= sun4i-csi/
+obj-y		+= sun50i-h713-hdmirx/
 obj-y		+= sun6i-csi/
 obj-y		+= sun6i-mipi-csi2/
 obj-y		+= sun8i-a83t-mipi-csi2/
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Kconfig b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Kconfig	1970-01-01 01:00:00.000000000 +0100
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Kconfig	2026-09-07 09:22:34.642945438 +0200
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+
+config VIDEO_SUN50I_H713_HDMIRX
+	tristate "Allwinner H713 HDMI receiver"
+	depends on V4L_PLATFORM_DRIVERS
+	depends on VIDEO_DEV
+	depends on ARCH_SUNXI || COMPILE_TEST
+	depends on HY310_CPU_COMM
+	depends on SUN50I_H713_ARISC
+	depends on DRM_SUN50I_H713_AFBD
+	select VIDEOBUF2_CORE
+	select VIDEOBUF2_MEMOPS
+	help
+	  V4L2 capture support for the HDMI receiver of the Allwinner H713.
+
+	  The receiver itself, the picture pipeline behind it and the capture
+	  DMA belong to the SoC's MIPS co-processor. This driver runs the
+	  firmware bring-up sequence over cpu_comm, drives EDID and hotplug
+	  through the ARISC, takes its "slot finished" events from the display
+	  driver's vsync, and hands the three capture slots the firmware fills
+	  out as V4L2 buffers without copying them.
+
+	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will
+	  be called sun50i-h713-hdmirx.
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Makefile b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Makefile
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Makefile	1970-01-01 01:00:00.000000000 +0100
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Makefile	2026-09-07 09:22:34.642964588 +0200
@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+obj-$(CONFIG_VIDEO_SUN50I_H713_HDMIRX) += sun50i-h713-hdmirx.o
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	1970-01-01 01:00:00.000000000 +0100
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 09:26:56.884438342 +0200
@@ -0,0 +1,1774 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * V4L2 capture driver for the HDMI receiver of the Allwinner H713.
+ *
+ * The H713 has no HDMI receiver the ARM can drive. The receiver, the whole
+ * picture pipeline behind it and the capture DMA belong to a MIPS
+ * co-processor that runs vendor firmware; the ARM reaches it through the
+ * cpu_comm IPC as a set of THal_Vp_* remote procedures, and it reaches the
+ * hotplug pin and the EDID store through a second co-processor, the ARISC.
+ * This driver owns neither the receiver nor the DMA. What it owns is the
+ * sequence that brings the firmware into the state in which it captures, and
+ * the bookkeeping that turns the ring the firmware writes into V4L2 buffers.
+ *
+ * Consequences that shape everything below:
+ *
+ *  - The capture target is not programmable. The firmware writes NV16 into
+ *    three fixed slot pairs inside its own framebuffer reservation and
+ *    rotates a pair of pointers through them. So a buffer cannot be
+ *    allocated: buffer i *is* slot i, handed to userspace by mmap, and
+ *    QBUF/DQBUF only pass ownership. Nothing is ever copied.
+ *
+ *  - There is no capture interrupt on the ARM. Three independent searches
+ *    through the firmware image found the "frame done" interrupt only inside
+ *    the MIPS interrupt controller; the stock device tree wires no capture
+ *    interrupt to the ARM at all, and the flip pointers are written by
+ *    hardware, not by firmware code (doku/84, RE question K2). What the ARM
+ *    does have is the display's vsync (GIC 142), where sun50i-h713-afbd reads
+ *    the flip pointers for its own passthrough plane. This driver takes its
+ *    "slot finished" events from there, through that driver's flip notifier,
+ *    and does not go near the register pair itself: the pair sits inside a
+ *    window display@5600000 owns and claims. The event still arrives behind
+ *    struct h713_hdmirx_slot_source, so a real capture interrupt could take
+ *    over one day without touching the queue handling.
+ *
+ *  - "Signal present" is a measurement, not a register bit we can read: on
+ *    signal loss the flip pointers stop moving and the last picture stays
+ *    (measured 07.09.2026, doku/nachtlog/K4-hotplug.md). QUERY_DV_TIMINGS
+ *    samples them for one frame period rather than trusting a cached flag.
+ *
+ *  - The firmware announces signal loss and signal return on its own through
+ *    the MipsHalCallback_SignalChange callback (same measurement). The V4L2
+ *    source change event hangs off that callback; nothing polls for it.
+ *
+ * Documentation: doku/88-v4l2-hdmirx.md, doku/78-nachtplan-hdmi-switch.md
+ * appendix A (the seven stages of a source change).
+ */
+
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/debugfs.h>
+#include <linux/firmware.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
+#include <linux/notifier.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/of_address.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/seq_file.h>
+#include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/spinlock.h>
+#include <linux/unaligned.h>
+#include <linux/v4l2-dv-timings.h>
+
+#include <linux/soc/sunxi/h713-afbd.h>
+#include <linux/soc/sunxi/h713-arisc.h>
+#include <linux/soc/sunxi/h713-cpu-comm.h>
+
+#include <media/v4l2-common.h>
+#include <media/v4l2-dev.h>
+#include <media/v4l2-device.h>
+#include <media/v4l2-dv-timings.h>
+#include <media/v4l2-event.h>
+#include <media/v4l2-fh.h>
+#include <media/v4l2-ioctl.h>
+#include <media/videobuf2-memops.h>
+#include <media/videobuf2-v4l2.h>
+
+#define H713_HDMIRX_NAME	"sun50i-h713-hdmirx"
+
+/*
+ * Geometry. The whole chain -- the firmware's window computation, the frame
+ * descriptor the display driver publishes, the AFBD crop -- is set up for
+ * 1920x1080p60 and nothing else has ever been measured on this board. A
+ * resolution change is an open point (doku/78 appendix A.6 item 4), which is
+ * why there is exactly one entry in the timings table below.
+ */
+#define H713_HDMIRX_WIDTH	1920
+#define H713_HDMIRX_HEIGHT	1080
+
+/* NV16: chroma at full height, so both planes are width * height bytes. */
+#define H713_HDMIRX_PLANES	2
+#define H713_HDMIRX_PLANE_SIZE	(H713_HDMIRX_WIDTH * H713_HDMIRX_HEIGHT)
+
+/*
+ * The capture ring. Three Y slots and three C slots, fixed by the firmware;
+ * the slot pitch is 0x1ff000 and C slot i is Y slot i plus 0x5fd000. Y and C
+ * always name the same index in every dump taken so far. Measured on the
+ * device, doku/76 sections 12 and 13; the addresses are checked against the
+ * "hdmi-ring" reserved region at probe, they are not trusted blindly.
+ */
+#define H713_HDMIRX_SLOTS	3
+
+/* Distance between two Y slots, and between Y slot i and C slot i. */
+#define H713_HDMIRX_SLOT_PITCH	0x1ff000
+#define H713_HDMIRX_SLOT_YC	0x5fd000
+
+static const phys_addr_t h713_hdmirx_slot_y[H713_HDMIRX_SLOTS] = {
+	0x4c3ef000, 0x4c5ee000, 0x4c7ed000,
+};
+
+static const phys_addr_t h713_hdmirx_slot_c[H713_HDMIRX_SLOTS] = {
+	0x4c9ec000, 0x4cbeb000, 0x4cdea000,
+};
+
+/*
+ * Offsets inside the cpu_comm shared memory. The MIPS dereferences whatever
+ * physical address it is handed; these two are where the stock tvserver puts
+ * the same things, so they are what this driver uses as well.
+ *
+ *   +0x036000  HDCP 2.2 key, 912 bytes  (stock session 32, Para[0] on the
+ *              wire is 0x4e336000)
+ *   +0x037000  the three Wce_SetWindow argument structures
+ *   +0x400000  staging area for THal_Vp_Init: its handler copies 55296 bytes
+ *              of its own .bss to the address in Para[2] without checking it,
+ *              so a zero there is a memcpy to NULL and takes the firmware
+ *              with it.
+ */
+#define H713_SHM_HDCP22		0x00036000
+#define H713_SHM_WCE		0x00037000
+#define H713_SHM_VP_INIT	0x00400000
+#define H713_SHM_WINDOW		0x00002000	/* covers HDCP22 and WCE */
+
+/* The same two, as offsets into the window that starts at H713_SHM_HDCP22. */
+#define H713_MAP_HDCP22		(H713_SHM_HDCP22 - H713_SHM_HDCP22)
+#define H713_MAP_WCE		(H713_SHM_WCE - H713_SHM_HDCP22)
+
+#define H713_HDCP22_SIZE	912
+#define H713_FW_HDCP22		"hy310-hdcp22.bin"
+
+/* Wce_SetWindow argument layout: src{4}, dst{4}, aspect{1}, each a pointer. */
+#define H713_WCE_SRC		0x000
+#define H713_WCE_DST		0x020
+#define H713_WCE_ASPECT		0x040
+#define H713_WCE_ASPECT_16_9	2
+
+/* THal_Vp_SetSource: 3 is HDMI-1, the board's only socket (hardware port 0). */
+#define H713_SOURCE_HDMI1	3
+#define H713_HDMI_PORT		0
+
+/*
+ * cpu_comm call timeouts. 0 means the API default of 500 ms, which is enough
+ * for every setter; the two calls that make the firmware move real data get
+ * their own budget. A call that runs out returns -ETIMEDOUT and the probe
+ * fails -- it is never retried, because a retry would hide the reason.
+ */
+#define H713_CALL_SLOW_MS	2000
+
+enum h713_step_kind {
+	H713_STEP_PLAIN,	/* arguments are the literals in the table */
+	H713_STEP_VP_INIT,	/* argument 2 is the staging address */
+	H713_STEP_WCE,		/* three shared-memory pointers */
+	H713_STEP_HDCP,		/* key address and key length */
+};
+
+/**
+ * struct h713_hdmirx_step - one call of the firmware bring-up sequence
+ * @name: routine name, hashed into the component id at run time
+ * @kind: how @args is turned into the argument words
+ * @nargs: number of argument words
+ * @args: the literal argument words for H713_STEP_PLAIN
+ * @session: session number of this call in the stock RPC trace, 0 if the call
+ *	is not part of the traced init (the three phase-2 calls)
+ * @timeout_ms: call timeout, 0 for the cpu_comm default
+ * @why: why the call is here and what its arguments mean
+ */
+struct h713_hdmirx_step {
+	const char *name;
+	enum h713_step_kind kind;
+	u8 nargs;
+	u32 args[3];
+	u16 session;
+	u16 timeout_ms;
+	const char *why;
+};
+
+/*
+ * The bring-up sequence, in stock order. This is stage 3 of the source change
+ * (doku/78 appendix A.2) and it is a table on purpose: the order is not a
+ * convention, it is what the firmware accepts, and prose invites reordering.
+ * The source is the stock RPC trace (re/captures/weltneuheit/stock-rpc-LIVE.txt,
+ * session numbers below) by way of analyse/hdmi-seq/hdmi_seq.py, which has run
+ * this sequence on the device.
+ *
+ * Stock session 16, SetWhiteBalance, is missing on purpose: it takes a pointer
+ * to three factory-calibrated doubles that this project does not have. The
+ * userspace tool skips it for the same reason and the sequence works without
+ * it.
+ */
+static const struct h713_hdmirx_step h713_hdmirx_init_seq[] = {
+	/* ---- phase 2: init and callback wiring ---------------------- */
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_Init", .kind = H713_STEP_VP_INIT, .nargs = 3,
+		.timeout_ms = H713_CALL_SLOW_MS,
+		.why = "ParaCount 3. Para[2] must be a valid staging address: "
+		       "the handler copies 55296 bytes of its own .bss there "
+		       "and does not check the pointer. Para[0] and Para[1] "
+		       "are not read.",
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_RegisterSignalChangeCallback",
+		.kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN, .nargs = 1, .args = { 11 },
+		.why = "ParaCount 1, the source id; 11 means all sources. The "
+		       "callback itself is found by the name hash of "
+		       "MipsHalCallback_SignalChange, not by an argument.",
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_SetHDMIHotPlugByPortCallback",
+		.kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN, .nargs = 1, .args = { 1 },
+		.why = "ParaCount 1, an enable flag -- 1 because this driver "
+		       "registers a handler for the hotplug callback.",
+	},
+	/* ---- phase 3: picture pipeline defaults --------------------- */
+	{
+		.name = "Thal_Vp_SetBacklightLevel", .kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN,
+		.nargs = 1, .args = { 0x64 }, .session = 14,
+		.why = "Lower case 'h' is the vendor's typo and part of the "
+		       "hashed name.",
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_SetBacklightWorkMode", .kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN,
+		.nargs = 1, .args = { 0 }, .session = 15,
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_SetTNR", .kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN,
+		.nargs = 1, .args = { 2 }, .session = 17,
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_SetSNR", .kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN,
+		.nargs = 1, .args = { 1 }, .session = 18,
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_SetDCI", .kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN,
+		.nargs = 1, .args = { 2 }, .session = 19,
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_SetBlackExtension", .kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN,
+		.nargs = 1, .args = { 1 }, .session = 20,
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_SetPictureMode", .kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN,
+		.nargs = 1, .args = { 1 }, .session = 21,
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_SetVideoRange", .kind = H713_STEP_PLAIN,
+		.nargs = 1, .args = { 0 }, .session = 22,
+	},
+	{
+		.name = "THal_Vp_Wce_SetWindow", .kind = H713_STEP_WCE,
+		.nargs = 3, .session = 23,
+		.why = "Three physical pointers: source window, destination "
+		       "window (both {h_start, h_size, v_start, v_size}) and "
